<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Dvina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Daugava</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Dvina, called the Daugava in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, rises in the Valdai Hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and Latvia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a trade route linking the Baltic with Rus', settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krivichs">Krivichs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a> grew on its banks, and Riga at its mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-dvina.jpg" alt="Western Dvina" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karlis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fluss-lv-D%C3%BCna.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Daugava, also known as the Western Dvina or the Väina River, rises in the Valdai Hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> close to the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. It flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. It is 1,020 kilometres long, of which 352 are in Latvia and 325 in Russia, and it traces a great south-bending curve through northern Belarus. Its catchment area is 87,900 square kilometres. Riga, the capital of Latvia, bridges the estuary four times, and its centre lies 15 kilometres from the river's mouth.</p><p>According to Max Vasmer's etymological dictionary, the name Dvina cannot come from a Uralic language and possibly derives from an Indo-European word for &quot;river&quot; or &quot;stream&quot;, resembling Danuvius, which comes from the Proto-Indo-European dānu, &quot;large river&quot;. The Finno-Ugric names, such as Livonian Vēna and Estonian Väina, stem from Proto-Finnic väin, roughly &quot;a large, peacefully rolling river&quot;.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have settled at the river's mouth and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> for millennia, at first as hunter-gatherers who fished the estuary. From about the sixth century CE Viking explorers crossed the Baltic and sailed upriver into the interior. In medieval times the Daugava formed part of the trade route from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> from the north and Byzantine silver from the south. The Riga area, inhabited by the Finnic-speaking Livs, was a key place of settlement and defence at the river's mouth at least as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, as the now destroyed fort at Torņakalns on the west bank shows.</p><p>From the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> much of the river formed the north-eastern border of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia, dividing it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, later Swedish Livonia. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> it separated the governorate of Courland on the west bank from Livonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a> on the east. The Ķegums Hydroelectric Power Station was built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> from 1936 to 1939, and the Pļaviņas station began work in 1968 and the Riga station in 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Towns on the river include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daugavpils">Daugavpils</a>, and Riga stands at its mouth. Polotsk is home to the Boris stones. The river's tributaries include the Mezha, the Dysna, the Aiviekste and the Ogre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daugava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By mid-January 1919 Soviet forces controlled about two thirds of the country, but the Lithuanian Army under General Silvestras Žukauskas held near Kėdainiai, retook Šiauliai, Radviliškis, Panevėžys and Ukmergė in the spring, and by the end of August had pushed the Soviets out and reached the <b>Daugava</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The archbishops were princes of the Holy Roman Empire, ruling lands along the <b>Daugava</b> and Gauja through vassals and castles such as Turaida and Kokenhusen.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a></li><li><blockquote>Its bishops controlled a few districts along the <b>Daugava</b>, depending on support from Riga and the Livonian Brothers of the Sword.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-semigallia">Bishopric of Semigallia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dregovichs settled the wet, forested lands between the Pripyat and the <b>Western Dvina</b> in the 7th and 8th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/dregovichs">Dregovichs</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Livonian Order dissolved in 1561, its last master, Gotthard Kettler, secularised the Order's lands south of the <b>Daugava</b> and became hereditary Duke of Courland and Semigallia, a vassal of Poland-Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li><li><blockquote>The lands north of the <b>Daugava</b> became the Duchy of Livonia, with the last master, Gotthard Kettler, as administrator.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-livonia">Duchy of Livonia</a></li></ul></section>
